WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.400
In American politics, there’s an idea that&nbsp;
the president basically determines the price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:26.720
of gas – that whatever you’re paying&nbsp;
at the pump, it’s always “their fault.”

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.200
But that’s not very accurate.&nbsp;
At the very simplest level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.200
the price of oil is pretty much a&nbsp;
reflection of supply and demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.680
The more oil there is on the market,&nbsp;
the less that oil is going to be worth.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.400
But of course, there’s more to energy markets&nbsp;
than just that. Think about who controls the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.520
energy market. Groups like the Organization&nbsp;
of Petroleum Exporting Countries, or OPEC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.840
decide how much oil is going to be pumped, in&nbsp;
order to basically set global prices. And energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.720
companies decide, based on market conditions,&nbsp;
how much oil they’re going to be producing.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.960
But unlike the consumers, who&nbsp;
of course want low prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.640
the suppliers have an incentive to keep&nbsp;
prices high – even in times of crisis.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.521
So oil companies could expand domestic production&nbsp;
to face the crisis. You’d think that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:32.080
make financial sense for them. But for some&nbsp;
reason, they’ve been very hesitant to do that.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.760
“Instead of ramping up production elsewhere&nbsp;
in the world investors and owners of these oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.440
companies are engaging in “capital discipline”,&nbsp;
according to the CEOs of these oil companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.440
and telling them that we don’t want&nbsp;
you to go out and drill more oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.720
because we don’t think that is going to&nbsp;
be profitable for us in the long term.”

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.040
“For the last ten years or so,&nbsp;
the financial community has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:03.120
given lots of money to these oil companies to&nbsp;
engage in exploration, especially in fracking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.320
and those investments turned out&nbsp;
actually to not be profitable.”

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.760
This is Matt Bruenig, of the People’s Policy&nbsp;
Project. He’s a policy analyst, with a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.240
focus on economics. And he’s pointing out&nbsp;
something that happened a few years ago.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.240
In the 2010s, investors lost money because&nbsp;
oil supply was too high, and prices were too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:33.440
low. Due to massive expansion of drilling,&nbsp;
American shale alone lost 300 billion dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.080
while motorists enjoyed what amounted to a&nbsp;
huge gas subsidy. So those investors learned:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.720
they now encourage oil companies not to&nbsp;
produce, in order to inflate profits.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.160
In the words of one financial&nbsp;
analyst: “It’s not the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.720
that is banning them from drilling more.&nbsp;
It’s pressure from their shareholders.”

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.080
That’s why a survey of oil and gas executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.160
found that the overwhelming reason for&nbsp;
low production was not government action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.920
but quote “investor pressure to&nbsp;
maintain capital discipline.”

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.600
So that’s the truth about why energy prices are&nbsp;
so high: it’s a system of upwards redistribution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.280
Gas companies are taking advantage of a crisis to&nbsp;
under-produce, in order to protect their profits.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.280
And the only way to prevent this is to&nbsp;
stop being so dependent on oil and gas.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.280
Just look at a country like France. In France,&nbsp;
nuclear power is the leading source of energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.120
housing is much denser, and public transportation&nbsp;
is decent. So the French have been relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.320
cushioned from the effects of oil price shocks,&nbsp;
simply because they aren’t so dependent on oil.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.840
Of course, switching your society’s built&nbsp;
environment away from oil requires a lot of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.960
So you need to think seriously about how to put&nbsp;
the oil industry into a sort of managed decline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.920
while also controlling prices for ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.160
car-driving consumers – who often&nbsp;
have no other choice but to drive.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.360
“We can’t have it go on forever because&nbsp;
obviously that would wreck the climate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.960
We also can’t end it immediately because we&nbsp;
don’t have enough green energy to replace it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.320
so the oil industry needs to&nbsp;
be put on a managed decline.”

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.960
But private investors are not in the business&nbsp;
of temporarily propping up industries they think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.040
are dying, in the face of huge efforts&nbsp;
to phase out fossil fuel extraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:35.200
They have no incentive to manage this transition&nbsp;
in a way that keeps ordinary people in mind.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.680
So if private actors are unable to manage this&nbsp;
transition, public ownership might be necessary.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.320
“By bringing public ownership you can manage both&nbsp;
ends of it. You can make sure that production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.520
continues when you need it and you can make&nbsp;
sure it goes away when we want it to go away.”

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.880
“Norway is an example that I use frequently,&nbsp;
they have a state-owned company which is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.120
called Equinor, and it has been running&nbsp;
pretty well. They were mostly concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.560
with capturing the profits of the industry&nbsp;
and making sure it serves the social good.”

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.720
Now, Norway is not perfect. In many ways,&nbsp;
Norway is very hypocritical about climate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.000
in large part because it is unwilling to&nbsp;
actually end its oil exploration activities.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.840
But if you’re interested in&nbsp;
controlling gas prices while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.360
also having the power to transition&nbsp;
away from fossil fuel dependence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.240
public actors are still much better&nbsp;
positioned to do that than private ones.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.760
It would mean managing oil and gas extraction&nbsp;
not through private actors motivated by profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.520
but public actors, using that money not for&nbsp;
stock buybacks and executive compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.720
but investment in green energy, dense housing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:06:05.840
and public transportation. A&nbsp;
better way is very possible.

